/*  measured01 -- a liquid MIXTURE property as a TABLE of measured rows (DEV.md
    4c C421 slice (8); docs/design/measured-facts-materials-and-flow-curves.md,
    form 1).

    The liquid is methanol + ethylene glycol.  Its viscosity is
    constant/experiments/royMethanolEthaneDiolViscosity.dat: 12 rows, three
    temperatures by four glycol mass fractions, `kind measured`, read from the
    article the file cites.  The entry below says HOW to read it --
    `interpolation ( logReciprocal monotoneCubic )`: along T, ln mu is linear
    in 1/T (the Arrhenius form); along w[ethyleneGlycol], the monotone cubic of
    Fritsch & Carlson -- one axis at a time, T first (the order of the file's
    columns).

    THREE DOMAINS (R2).  The SUPPORT is computed from the rows: T 293.15-303.15
    K, w[ethyleneGlycol] 0-0.75; inside it the table interpolates, and on a row
    it returns the row's own value.  The AUTHORISED box is wider (T 290-306 K,
    w 0-0.80): between the support and it the value of the nearest edge is held
    and the largest excursion announced, at the end of the run.  Outside the
    authorised box the run REFUSES -- a table is never extrapolated nor handed
    over to a law.

    The pure components keep their record laws (a pure liquid is not a mixture:
    the table answers only where both components are present).  */

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 1;

components ( methanol ethyleneGlycol );

equilibrium
{
    formulation gammaPhi;
    liquid { activityModel { model ideal; } }
    vapour { fugacityModel idealGas; }
}

transport
{
    liquid
    {
        viscosity
        {
            measured
            {
                royMethanolEthaneDiolViscosity
                {
                    use            method;
                    interpolation  ( logReciprocal monotoneCubic );     // T, then w[ethyleneGlycol]
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
